Third-party work
================

The Twitch emote runtime (extensions/twitch/src/main/java/io/github/sabopura/morphe/extension/twitch/chat/emotes),
channel/chat-row anchor design, deleted-message, points, notification and login patches derive from Hooman's Morphe Patches,
https://github.com/arandomhooman/hoomans-morphe-patches,
revision 018277825a611ad61f3a16a70e562291c06f4081, licensed under GPLv3.
Changes add FFZ, provider switches, CDN validation, lifecycle fixes, overlays,
image limits, an emote picker, and integration with independently implemented
chat and player settings. Points now use semantic model/contract resolution,
native eligibility results, deferred/coalesced execution and bounded retries.
Notification correction is endpoint-scoped and preserves header API semantics;
the optional login workaround returns the native Kotlin Unit instance.

The Twitch "Block ads" patch was implemented from an analysis of Twitch 31.5.2. It follows
techniques published by:
- ryykitty/twitch-patched (https://github.com/ryykitty/twitch-patched), GPLv3: wrapping the
  Amazon IVS HTTP client, making its callback interfaces public, and answering the player
  with a rebuilt playlist response.
- bakwudo/uyu (https://github.com/bakwudo/uyu), GPLv3: starting the ad request presenter in
  its disabled state, the picture-by-picture feature check, covering an ad that is part of
  the stream, opening live streams in the native player screen, and the row in Twitch's
  settings screen.
- TwitchAdSolutions and TwitchAdBlock (MIT), through the above: fetching playlists of other
  player types while an ad is stitched into the stream.

